Spermatocele
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Painless scrotal swelling, Mass superior/posterior to testicle, Brilliant transillumination, Anechoic cyst on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-042
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe psychological panic following the incidental discovery of a scrotal mass, requiring immediate clinical assessment to rule out testicular malignancy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing an acute anxiety crisis and hyperventilation after discovering a firm mass in their scrotum. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to perform a definitive physical examination, accurately differentiate a benign cystic lesion from a solid malignant tumor, and immediately de-escalate the severe psychiatric distress that could not safely wait for a routine daytime appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was in the shower and found a hard lump right above my testicle, and I am absolutely terrified it is cancer.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports the incidental discovery of a painless, insidious unilateral swelling within the scrotum thirty minutes prior to the call. They describe a readily palpable, firm, and spherical mass located distinctly superior and posterior to the left testicle. They explicitly deny any scrotal erythema, warmth, or localized tenderness. There is a complete lack of dysuria, urethral discharge, or signs of urinary tract infection. The mass remains constant in size and does not change with straining or standing.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently pacing the room, hyperventilating, and exhibiting severe acute health anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme psychological distress; otherwise low social risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.7C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, breathing rapidly, speaking in a hurried and panicked tone.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary: Examination reveals a smooth, tense, spherical cystic mass measuring approximately 2 centimeters, located strictly at the superior pole of the epididymis. The examiner is able to clearly palpate the normal, firm testicle completely separate from the cystic mass. Brilliant transillumination is observed when a focused light source is applied directly to the swelling. No inguinal lymphadenopathy is present. Expected investigation results will demonstrate a distinct, anechoic, fluid-filled simple cystic structure arising from the epididymal head on a targeted scrotal ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Laptop open to internet search pages detailing testicular cancer symptoms.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications currently taken.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Spermatocele - Status: Stable (with acute anxiety)
Differentials:

Testicular Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to the mass being distinctly separate from the testicle itself, situated superiorly, and demonstrating brilliant transillumination indicating fluid rather than solid malignant tissue.

Epididymitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of localized tenderness, induration, erythema, and infectious urinary symptoms.

Varicocele - Ruled out/unlikely as the mass is a distinct, spherical cyst rather than a compressible, tortuous bag of worms that enlarges with the Valsalva maneuver.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents in a state of acute panic, misinterpreting a benign epididymal cyst as a terminal malignancy. The defining physical characteristics of a spermatocele separating clearly from the testicle and transilluminating brightly confirm its benign, fluid-filled nature. An urgent after-hours physical examination was absolutely critical to definitively exclude the presence of a solid malignant mass and physically demonstrate the benign findings to the patient. This immediate diagnostic exclusion was necessary to arrest a severe anxiety crisis that was causing significant autonomic distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough, guided physical examination alongside the patient, demonstrating the transillumination and the distinct separation of the mass from the testicle to provide immediate, definitive reassurance. Generated a referral for a routine, non-urgent scrotal ultrasound for formal anatomical documentation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To eliminate the anxiety-inducing habit of internet symptom checking, the patient is instructed to physically place their laptop in another room immediately after noting any minor physical symptom, committing to a 24-hour waiting period before researching. Experiencing a natural reduction in panic during this waiting period reinforces the habit of delayed reaction, breaking the immediate cycle of catastrophic health anxiety.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in two weeks to review the ultrasound report and confirm the benign nature of the cyst.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate medical review is required only if the mass suddenly becomes exquisitely painful, rapidly doubles in size, or if the overlying skin turns red and hot.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical examination strongly confirms a benign fluid collection, but a routine ultrasound is standard practice to confirm this definitively.
Clinical Course: The cyst is completely harmless and requires no surgical intervention unless it grows large enough to cause significant mechanical discomfort over the coming years.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If the lump becomes severely painful, hot, or rapidly enlarges, you must seek medical evaluation to rule out an infection or bleeding into the cyst.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have found a spermatocele, which is simply a small, harmless cyst filled with fluid located in the tubes just above the testicle. Because I can feel your normal testicle entirely separate from it, and because light shines straight through it, we know it is not a solid tumor or cancer. There is no emergency.
Actionable Steps: Stop researching your symptoms online. Attend the routine ultrasound appointment next week. Do not continuously squeeze or manipulate the cyst, as this can cause traumatic inflammation.

